.TH "BCR" "1" "" "" "Breadcrumb SDD"
.SH "NAME"
.PP
bcr \- audit the breadcrumbs of a repository
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr audit\-report \-\-html [\-o FILE]
bcr check
bcr verdict [ID|PATH|\-]...
bcr list [ID|PATH|\-]...
bcr links [\-r] [ID|PATH|\-]...
bcr new TYPE TITLE [PARENT|\-]...
.fi
.RE
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBbcr\fR is the toolkit of Breadcrumb SDD. It reads the breadcrumbs of a repository, checks the claims of its Tasks against the repository's files, and reports what it finds. Run it from the root of the repository.
.PP
This document is the contract of the interface of \fBbcr\fR: its commands, flags, inputs, outputs and exit codes. The rules of the method are in the breadcrumbs of the Breadcrumb SDD repository, and this document points to the one that holds each rule (TD\-0018).
.PP
Every command follows the same conventions:
.IP \(bu 2
It is run as \fBbcr COMMAND [FLAGS] [OPERANDS]\fR. Flags come before operands; the first operand, or \fB\-\-\fR, ends them (TD\-0020).
.IP \(bu 2
Flags are in getopt style: a short flag is one letter, such as \fB\-a\fR, and short flags can be combined, as in \fB\-ab\fR; a long flag is a name, such as \fB\-\-html\fR. A flag's value follows it: \fB\-x VALUE\fR, \fB\-xVALUE\fR, \fB\-\-name=VALUE\fR or \fB\-\-name VALUE\fR (RQ\-0011).
.IP \(bu 2
Results go to standard output, and nothing else does. Messages go to standard error and start with \fBbcr: \fR. A usage error is followed by the usage line (RQ\-0009, TD\-0020).
.IP \(bu 2
The exit status says whether the command found something wrong (see EXIT STATUS).
.IP \(bu 2
A command that takes breadcrumbs as operands takes an id, such as \fBTK\-0001\fR, a path relative to the repository root, such as \fBbreadcrumbs/TK\-0001.md\fR, or \fB\-\fR, which reads one breadcrumb per line of standard input, named by the line's first tab\-separated field. With no operands, it takes every breadcrumb, in order of path. An operand that names no breadcrumb is written to standard error, the others are still taken, and the command exits with 2 (TD\-0026, RQ\-0012, RQ\-0025).
.PP
Which files are breadcrumbs: TD\-0013. The kinds of breadcrumb: TD\-0001, TD\-0003, TD\-0004 and TD\-0011. How a breadcrumb names its parents: TD\-0005.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.SS "audit\-report"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr audit\-report \-\-html [\-o FILE]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Audits the breadcrumbs and writes the result as an HTML report (RQ\-0001).
.PP
Flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-html\fR: write the report as HTML. It is required, and HTML is the only format.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-o FILE\fR, \fB\-\-output FILE\fR: write the report to \fBFILE\fR instead of \fBaudit\-report.html\fR, replacing any file there. A relative \fBFILE\fR is taken from the current directory. \fB\-o \-\fR writes the report to standard output (RQ\-0017, RQ\-0018).
.PP
Operands: none.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: without \fB\-o\fR, the report is written to \fBaudit\-report.html\fR in the current directory, replacing any file there (RQ\-0019). When the report is written to a file, standard output gets one line, the absolute path of the report. With \fB\-o \-\fR, standard output gets the report, and no file is written.
.PP
The report draws the breadcrumbs as a graph (RQ\-0002), each with its properties (RQ\-0003) and an arrow to each of its parents (RQ\-0004), colored by its verdict (RQ\-0005). How a verdict is reached: TD\-0012 for a claim, RQ\-0007 for a Task, and RQ\-0006 and RQ\-0008 for the breadcrumbs above it. The report lists the same problems as \fBbcr check\fR, each with its file and line.
.PP
Exit status:
.IP \(bu 2
0: the report is written, and no breadcrumb is Refuted or has a problem.
.IP \(bu 2
1: the report is written, and a breadcrumb is Refuted or has a problem.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory, or the report could not be written, such as to a directory that does not exist. No report is written.
.SS "check"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr check
.fi
.RE
.PP
Checks the syntax of the breadcrumbs and prints each problem it finds (RQ\-0020).
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: none. Every breadcrumb is checked.
.PP
Standard input: not read.
.PP
Output: one line on standard output for each problem, in order of path and then of line:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
PATH:LINE: MESSAGE
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBPATH\fR is relative to the repository root, and \fBLINE\fR is counted from 1 (TD\-0020). What is a problem: a first line that is not a title (TD\-0013), a \fBParent:\fR line that is not a link or links to no breadcrumb (TD\-0005, RQ\-0022), and a list item under a Task's \fB## Claims\fR heading that is not a claim (TD\-0011, TD\-0012). A claim that does not hold is not a problem; \fBbcr audit\-report\fR shows it in the Task's verdict.
.PP
Standard error gets a warning for each file in \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR that is not a breadcrumb (RQ\-0023):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr: warning: PATH is not a breadcrumb (TD\-0013)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0021):
.IP \(bu 2
0: no problem was found. Warnings do not change it.
.IP \(bu 2
1: a problem was found.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory.
.SS "verdict"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr verdict [ID|PATH|\-]...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prints the verdict of each breadcrumb (RQ\-0024), or of the breadcrumbs it is given (RQ\-0025).
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: breadcrumbs, as DESCRIPTION defines, in the order given (RQ\-0025).
.PP
Standard input: read when an operand is \fB\-\fR. The output of any command here can feed it.
.PP
Output: one line on standard output for each breadcrumb: its id, a tab, and its verdict, \fBConfirmed\fR, \fBRefuted\fR or \fBUndecided\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
TK\-0001	Confirmed
.fi
.RE
.PP
How a verdict is reached: see audit\-report.
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0026):
.IP \(bu 2
0: no breadcrumb printed is Refuted. Undecided ones do not change it.
.IP \(bu 2
1: a breadcrumb printed is Refuted.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, an operand names no breadcrumb, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr verdict | grep \-w Refuted | cut \-f1
bcr verdict breadcrumbs/TK\-*.md
.fi
.RE
.SS "list"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr list [ID|PATH|\-]...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prints each breadcrumb with its type and title (RQ\-0027).
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands: breadcrumbs, as DESCRIPTION defines, in the order given (RQ\-0029).
.PP
Standard input: read when an operand is \fB\-\fR.
.PP
Output: one line on standard output for each breadcrumb: its id, its type and its title, separated by tabs. The type is \fBIntake\fR, \fBRequirement\fR, \fBTechnical Decision\fR or \fBTask\fR.
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0029):
.IP \(bu 2
0: the breadcrumbs were printed.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, an operand names no breadcrumb, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory.
.SS "links"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr links [\-r] [ID|PATH|\-]...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prints each link from a breadcrumb to its parent (RQ\-0028).
.PP
Flags:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-recursive\fR: also print the links of each parent reached, and of their parents, up to the breadcrumbs with none. Each link is printed once, the first time it is reached (RQ\-0030).
.PP
Operands: breadcrumbs, as DESCRIPTION defines. Only the links they have are printed, in the order given (RQ\-0029).
.PP
Standard input: read when an operand is \fB\-\fR.
.PP
Output: one line on standard output for each \fBParent:\fR link: the id of the breadcrumb that has it, a tab, and the id of the parent, in order of the breadcrumb's \fBParent:\fR lines. A link to no breadcrumb prints the path it points to in place of the parent's id; \fBbcr check\fR reports it as a problem. How a breadcrumb names its parents: TD\-0005.
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0029):
.IP \(bu 2
0: the links were printed.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, an operand names no breadcrumb, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory.
.PP
Examples: what a Refuted Task breaks, with titles:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr verdict | grep \-w Refuted | bcr links \-r \- | cut \-f2 | sort \-u | bcr list \-
.fi
.RE
.SS "new"
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr new TYPE TITLE [PARENT|\-]...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Creates a breadcrumb in \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR and prints its path (RQ\-0031).
.PP
Flags: none.
.PP
Operands:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTYPE\fR: \fBIN\fR, \fBRQ\fR, \fBTD\fR or \fBTK\fR, the prefix of the breadcrumb's kind (TD\-0027).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTITLE\fR: its title, on one line. Quote it in the shell.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPARENT\fR: each breadcrumb it derives from, as DESCRIPTION defines; with none, it has no parent (RQ\-0033).
.PP
Standard input: read when a \fBPARENT\fR is \fB\-\fR.
.PP
Output: the path of the new breadcrumb, relative to the repository root, on standard output. Its number is one more than the highest of its type (TD\-0027). It holds its title line, a \fBParent:\fR line for each parent, and, for a Technical Decision or a Task, the empty sections to fill in (RQ\-0032).
.PP
A breadcrumb made by \fBbcr new\fR is a draft. Who approves it before it is committed: TD\-0002 and TD\-0006.
.PP
Exit status (RQ\-0033):
.IP \(bu 2
0: the breadcrumb was written.
.IP \(bu 2
2: \fBbcr\fR was used wrongly, the type or title is not valid, a parent names no breadcrumb, no number is left, the file could not be written, or there is no \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR directory in the current directory. Nothing is written, and no file is ever replaced.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
bcr new TD "Use a cache" IN\-0003
bcr verdict | grep \-w Undecided | grep '^RQ' | bcr new TK "Carry them out" \-
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.PP
Every command exits with (RQ\-0010):
.IP \(bu 2
0: it succeeded and found nothing wrong.
.IP \(bu 2
1: it ran and found something wrong in the breadcrumbs.
.IP \(bu 2
2: it was used wrongly, or could not run.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
The method: \fBREADME.md\fR. How the repository is developed: \fBCONTRIBUTING.md\fR. The rules: the breadcrumbs in \fBbreadcrumbs/\fR.
